<p>Susan Haack, professor of philosophy and law at the University of Miami, has died.<span id="more-56839"></span></p>
<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class=" wp-image-56840 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/haack-susan.jpg" alt="" width="623" height="350" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/haack-susan.jpg 803w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/haack-susan-300x168.jpg 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/haack-susan-768x431.jpg 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/haack-susan-400x225.jpg 400w" sizes="(max-width: 623px) 100vw, 623px" /></p>
<p><a href="https://people.miami.edu/profile/b56df3f60a56e232d77e4f005d8aa34b" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Professor Haack</a> was well-known for her work in philosophy of logic, epistemology, pragmatism, and philosophy of law. She is the author of <em><a href="http://press.uchicago.edu/ucp/books/book/chicago/D/bo3683514.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Deviant Logic, Fuzzy Logic</a> </em>(1974), <em><a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/philosophy-of-logics/951150505337A200EB152237BDF55A3B" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philosophy of Logics</a> </em>(1978), <a href="https://www.amazon.com/Evidence-Inquiry-Pragmatist-Reconstruction-Epistemology/dp/159102689X?crid=38Z53OO8YECXS&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.HVCzVif7pxw9kN7RzHxhCarG40XQ99zmO9sshlU0Tt_GjHj071QN20LucGBJIEps.uNi4bDydLfhmkoYQSikgq3Ex9sRqkeFWaaFrWXf0A8Y&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=evidence+and+inquiry+haack&amp;qid=1773285540&amp;sprefix=evidence+and+inquiry+haack%2Caps%2C187&amp;sr=8-1&amp;linkCode=ll2&amp;tag=dainou-20&amp;linkId=0104237ba139d4e076ca92dc7859a8a2&amp;language=en_US&amp;ref_=as_li_ss_tl" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>Evidence and Inquiry: Towards Reconstruction in Epistemology</em></a> (1993, revised 2009), <a href="https://press.uchicago.edu/ucp/books/book/chicago/M/bo3614193.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>Manifesto of a Passionate Moderate</em></a> (2000), <a href="https://www.simonandschuster.com/books/Putting-Philosophy-to-Work/Susan-Haack/9781616144937" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>Putting Philosophy to Work: Inquiry and Its Place in Culture</em></a> (2013), <em><a href="https://www.simonandschuster.com/books/Defending-Science-within-Reason/Susan-Haack/9781615921683" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Defending Science&#8212;Within Reason</a> </em>(2011), <a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/evidence-matters/3E642B4CBD8CD6D2EF5A6A5615324797" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>Evidence Matters: Science, Proof, and Truth in the Law</em></a> (2014), among many <a href="https://philpeople.org/profiles/susan-haack" target="_blank" rel="noopener">other works</a>.</p>
<p>She joined the philosophy faculty at the University of Miami in 1990, was appointed as a professor of law there in 2000, and was named a distinguished professor in the humanities in 2006. Prior to arriving at Miami, she was a professor at the University of Warwick. She earned her PhD from the University of Cambridge, an MA from Oxford and Cambridge, and a BPhil and BA from Oxford. Over the years she held visiting appointments at many institutions around the world&#8212;in Brazil, Colombia, Poland, Spain, the UK, Mexico, Italy, Denmark, Australia, Canada, and the US. Among her various honors, she delivered over 30 named or endowed lectures over the course of her career.</p>
<p>Professor Haack died on March 10th, 2026.</p>
